            ## SCENARIO NARRATIVE:
            ## Phase 1: Initial Access (T1190)
A threat actor discovers a public-facing web application running React Server Components
(Next.js RSC) on Azure App Service, exposed through Azure Front Door. The attacker identifies
the /_next/rsc endpoint and crafts a malicious RSC payload exploiting a prototype pollution
vulnerability (CVE-2025-55182 class). The payload manipulates Object.prototype to achieve
arbitrary code execution within the Node.js server process. FrontDoorAccessLog records the
anomalous POST request to /_next/rsc with an oversized JSON body. AppServiceHTTPLogs capture
the 200 response confirming successful exploitation.

Key log evidence:
- FrontDoorAccessLog: HttpMethod=POST, RequestUri=/_next/rsc, ClientIp=attacker, RequestBytes>5000, HttpStatusCode=200
- FrontDoorWAFLog: Action=Allow (WAF in Detection mode failed to block)
- AppServiceHTTPLogs: CsUriStem=/_next/rsc, CsMethod=POST, ScStatus=200, anomalous CsBytes

## Phase 2: Persistence (T1505.003)
With RCE achieved, the attacker deploys a file-based web shell to the App Service filesystem.
The web shell is written to a path under /home/site/wwwroot/ disguised as a legitimate
application file (e.g., api/diagnostics.js). This provides persistent command execution
access that survives application restarts. Subsequent requests to the web shell endpoint
appear in AppServiceHTTPLogs with distinctive patterns — POST requests to the new endpoint
with command parameters.

Key log evidence:
- AppServiceHTTPLogs: CsUriStem=/api/diagnostics, CsMethod=POST, ScStatus=200, repeated from same CIp
- AppServiceConsoleLogs: File write operations to /home/site/wwwroot/api/diagnostics.js

## Phase 3: Credential Access (T1552.005)
Through the web shell, the attacker queries the Azure Instance Metadata Service (IMDS) at
http://169.254.169.254/metadata/identity/oauth2/token with the Metadata:true header and
resource=https://management.azure.com/. The IMDS endpoint returns a managed identity OAuth2
bearer token. A second token request targets resource=https://storage.azure.com/ for direct
storage access. AzureIMDSAccessLogs record both token requests.

Key log evidence:
- AzureIMDSAccessLogs: Resource=https://management.azure.com/, StatusCode=200, Identity=system-assigned MI
- AzureIMDSAccessLogs: Resource=https://storage.azure.com/, StatusCode=200
- AADSignInLogs: ServicePrincipalId=MI_OBJECT_ID, ResourceDisplayName=Windows Azure Service Management API
- AppServiceHTTPLogs: Correlated outbound activity timing with IMDS request

## Phase 4: Discovery (T1580)
Armed with the managed identity token, the attacker performs ARM API reconnaissance. They
enumerate subscriptions (GET /subscriptions), resource groups
(GET /subscriptions/{sub}/resourcegroups), and storage accounts
(GET /subscriptions/{sub}/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts).
AzureActivityLogs capture each ARM API call with the managed identity's object ID in the
authorization Claims field.

Key log evidence:
- AzureActivityLogs: OperationNameValue=Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/read, Claims.objectidentifier=MI_OBJECT_ID
- AzureActivityLogs: OperationNameValue=Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resourceGroups/read (rapid sequential)
- AzureActivityLogs: OperationNameValue=Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/read, ResultType=Success
- AADSignInLogs: ServicePrincipalId=MI_OBJECT_ID, ResourceDisplayName=Windows Azure Service Management API

## Phase 5: Collection (T1530)
Using the managed identity token with Storage Blob Data Reader permissions, the attacker
accesses Azure Blob Storage. They enumerate containers (ListContainers), list blobs
(ListBlobs), and download sensitive files (GetBlob) containing financial records, HR
documents, and customer data. StorageBlobLogs record all operations with the managed
identity as the authenticated caller (RequesterObjectId matches MI object ID).

Key log evidence:
- StorageBlobLogs: OperationName=ListContainers, AuthenticationType=OAuth, RequesterObjectId=MI_OBJECT_ID
- StorageBlobLogs: OperationName=ListBlobs, ContainerName=financial-reports, StatusCode=200
- StorageBlobLogs: OperationName=GetBlob, multiple blobs, high ResponseBodySize
- StorageBlobLogs: CallerIpAddress matches App Service outbound IP
- AADSignInLogs: ServicePrincipalId=MI_OBJECT_ID, ResourceDisplayName=Azure Storage

## Detection Summary
Cross-table correlation opportunities:
1. FrontDoorAccessLog.ClientIp → AppServiceHTTPLogs.CIp (same attacker IP through Front Door)
2. AzureIMDSAccessLogs.ObjectId → AzureActivityLogs.Claims.objectidentifier → StorageBlobLogs.RequesterObjectId (same MI across all phases)
3. AADSignInLogs.CorrelationId → AzureActivityLogs.CorrelationId (linked ARM operations)
4. Timeline: RSC exploit → web shell deploy → IMDS call → ARM enum → blob exfil (sequential within minutes)

